import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Module: handl_egraph","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"api/egraph/handl_egraph/handl_egraph.md","filePath":"api/egraph/handl_egraph/handl_egraph.md"}`),a={name:`api/egraph/handl_egraph/handl_egraph.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<p><strong>handl_egraph</strong></p><h1 id="module-handl-egraph" tabindex="-1">Module: handl_egraph <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-handl-egraph" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: handl_egraph”">​</a></h1><h2 id="contents" tabindex="-1">Contents <a class="header-anchor" href="#contents" aria-label="Permalink to “Contents”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Modules</strong></p><ul><li><a href="#egraph"><code>egraph</code></a> - The e-graph: union-find interned representation, matching, and</li><li><a href="#extract"><code>extract</code></a> - Extraction: deterministic minimum-cost member selection.</li><li><a href="#node"><code>node</code></a> - E-nodes and e-classes: the interned node representation.</li><li><a href="#pattern"><code>pattern</code></a> - Compiled patterns — the spec&#39;s <code>_PVar</code>/<code>_PLeaf</code>/<code>_POp</code>/<code>_Prog</code>.</li><li><a href="#rewrite"><code>rewrite</code></a> - Rewrite rules, side-condition environments, and provenance records.</li><li><a href="#subst"><code>subst</code></a> - Substitutions: metavariable bindings produced by a match.</li><li><a href="#term"><code>term</code></a> - The crate&#39;s term type — patterns and concrete terms share it.</li><li><a href="#term-bridge"><code>term_bridge</code></a> - The kernel bridge: <code>hnl_engine::Term</code> / <code>serde_json::Value</code> ↔</li><li><a href="#union-find"><code>union_find</code></a> - Union-find (disjoint-set): path halving + union by rank.</li><li><a href="#value"><code>value</code></a> - Attribute values: the hashable, totally-ordered scalar/composite</li></ul><hr><h2 id="module-egraph" tabindex="-1">Module: egraph <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-egraph" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: egraph”">​</a></h2><p>The e-graph: union-find interned representation, matching, and bounded equality saturation.</p><p>Ports the spec&#39;s <code>EGraph</code> (<code>core.py</code>) with three deliberate divergences, all named:</p><ul><li><strong>Eager per-class match enumeration.</strong> The spec streams matches through a frozen-read <code>_Epoch</code> so <code>apply_rule</code> can union between yielded substitutions while the enumeration keeps its call-time view. An eager per-class enumeration is the <em>same</em> observable semantics — the epoch machinery exists to reproduce exactly the list the pre-streaming matcher returned. We enumerate that list directly; memory stays bounded per class by the same [<code>EGraph::MATCH_CAP</code>] the spec applies.</li><li><strong>Structural ordering</strong> (<code>ENode: Ord</code>) replaces the set + <code>_member_key</code> rescan, and a typed [<code>Subst</code>] replaces the <code>&quot;$attr:&quot;</code> key-mangling. Same total order, same determinism.</li><li><strong><code>check</code>/<code>derive</code> see a resolved [<code>BoundEnv</code>]</strong> computed before the hook runs — the hooks themselves are pure, so they can never mutate the graph mid-application.</li></ul><h2 id="module-extract" tabindex="-1">Module: extract <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-extract" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: extract”">​</a></h2><p>Extraction: deterministic minimum-cost member selection.</p><p>Ports the spec&#39;s <code>extract_best</code> core — the DAG-aware greedy walk — minus its domain payload: no <code>FOLD_OPS</code> param-only discount, no fusion tie-break, no roofline/overhead memo probes (those are cost-model policies the domain injects, not substrate). What stays is exactly the contract the domain relies on:</p><ul><li>members are scanned in canonical ([<code>ENode</code>]) order;</li><li>each e-class in the extracted DAG is charged <strong>once</strong> (the <code>used</code>-set billing — a shared child contributes its subtree cost only for classes not already accounted for);</li><li><code>local = max(cost(term) - Σ cost(children), 0)</code>;</li><li>ties resolve on fewest ops (<code>nops</code>), then canonical order;</li><li>cyclic classes are unextractable;</li><li><code>overrides</code>/<code>bans</code> steer the pick (coordinated extraction and bounded-ε filtering plug in here).</li></ul><p><code>extract_min_depth</code>, <code>dag_cost</code>, <code>extract_alternatives</code>, and <code>diverse_classes</code> port verbatim modulo the same domain strip.</p><h2 id="module-node" tabindex="-1">Module: node <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-node" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: node”">​</a></h2><p>E-nodes and e-classes: the interned node representation.</p><p>An [<code>ENode</code>] is a <code>(op, children, attrs)</code> triple where children are <em>canonical</em> e-class ids. Ordering is structural — <code>(op, children, attrs)</code> lexicographic — which <em>is</em> the spec&#39;s <code>_member_key</code> contract: a total, process-independent member order consumed by every decision-reaching member scan (matching order, congruence drain, <code>any_term</code>, extraction tie-breaks). The spec computed it as <code>(op, children, repr(attrs))</code>; structural <code>Ord</code> keeps the invariant without the repr (named divergence — strictly better complexity, same determinism guarantee).</p><h2 id="module-pattern" tabindex="-1">Module: pattern <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-pattern" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: pattern”">​</a></h2><p>Compiled patterns — the spec&#39;s <code>_PVar</code>/<code>_PLeaf</code>/<code>_POp</code>/<code>_Prog</code>.</p><p>Each rule&#39;s pattern compiles <strong>once per e-graph</strong> into a <code>PNode</code> tree carrying everything the match needs precomputed: the normalised attr tuple, the attr-name keyset for the exact-equality gate, the prebuilt leaf [<code>ENode</code>] for the registry probe, and the per-child op constraints feeding targeted eligibility (<code>_opk</code>).</p><h2 id="module-rewrite" tabindex="-1">Module: rewrite <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-rewrite" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: rewrite”">​</a></h2><p>Rewrite rules, side-condition environments, and provenance records.</p><p>Ports the spec&#39;s <code>Rewrite</code> minus its domain-law bookkeeping (<code>derivation</code>/<code>cond</code>/<code>dspec</code> are <code>catopt_core.laws.cond</code> machinery — the crate keeps the generic core: patterns, <code>check</code>/<code>derive</code> hooks, tags, and the ε-law bound fields).</p><h2 id="module-subst" tabindex="-1">Module: subst <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-subst" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: subst”">​</a></h2><p>Substitutions: metavariable bindings produced by a match.</p><p>The spec keeps one flat dict with two namespaces — term metavariables under their own names, attribute metavariables under <code>&quot;$attr:&lt;name&gt;&quot;</code> keys. The Rust port splits the namespaces into two ordered maps (named divergence — typed beats key-mangled), which is also what <code>derive</code> hooks and the recorded <code>ProofEdge</code> subst see.</p><h2 id="module-term" tabindex="-1">Module: term <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-term" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: term”">​</a></h2><p>The crate&#39;s term type — patterns and concrete terms share it.</p><p>Mirrors the spec&#39;s dual use of <code>Op</code> trees: a rule&#39;s <code>lhs</code>/<code>rhs</code>, the terms interned into the graph, and the terms extraction produces are all [<code>Term</code>]. Two positions are pattern-only:</p><ul><li>[<code>Term::Var</code>] — a metavariable binding an e-class (the spec&#39;s bare <code>str</code> leaf).</li><li>[<code>TermAttr::Meta</code>] — an attribute metavariable (the spec&#39;s string-valued attr, bound under the <code>&quot;$attr:&quot;</code> namespace — the separate [<code>crate::Subst::attrs</code>] map replaces that key-mangling).</li></ul><p><code>EGraph::add_term</code> rejects both — a metavariable is not a term — where the spec would silently leaf-intern a <code>str</code> (named wart).</p><h2 id="module-term-bridge" tabindex="-1">Module: term_bridge <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-term-bridge" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: term_bridge”">​</a></h2><p>The kernel bridge: <code>hnl_engine::Term</code> / <code>serde_json::Value</code> ↔ the crate&#39;s [<code>Term</code>].</p><p>Encoding contract — every <code>Term</code> variant maps to an <code>Op</code> node whose <code>op</code> is the variant tag (except <code>Term::Op</code>, whose enode op is the domain operator <code>name</code>), with <code>Term</code>-typed fields as child positions in declaration order and scalar/<code>Value</code> fields as literal attrs. <code>Term::Op</code> additionally encodes <code>dim</code> as <strong>child position 0</strong> so rewrites can reach it; <code>source</code>/<code>lit</code> ride attrs:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th><code>Term</code> variant</th><th>enode <code>op</code></th><th>children</th><th>attrs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>Arr{dim, value}</code></td><td><code>arr</code></td><td><code>[dim]</code></td><td><code>value</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Const{value}</code></td><td>— <em>leaf</em></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>self-describing key</td></tr><tr><td><code>Op{dim,name,args,source,lit}</code></td><td><code>name</code></td><td><code>[dim, ..args]</code></td><td><code>source</code>, <code>foreign</code>?, <code>lit</code>?</td></tr><tr><td><code>Seq{axis,fst,snd}</code></td><td><code>seq</code></td><td><code>[axis,fst,snd]</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>First{inner}</code></td><td><code>first</code></td><td><code>[inner]</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>Choice{l,r}</code></td><td><code>choice</code></td><td><code>[l,r]</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>Loop{body}</code></td><td><code>loop</code></td><td><code>[body]</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>App{callee,args}</code></td><td><code>app</code></td><td><code>[callee, ..args]</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>Iter{fuel,predicate,step,initial}</code></td><td><code>iter</code></td><td>4 children</td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>Child{role}</code></td><td><code>child</code></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td><code>role</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Handle{body,target,impl_}</code></td><td><code>handle</code></td><td><code>[body,impl_]</code></td><td><code>target</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Rewrite{source,target,witness}</code></td><td><code>rewrite</code></td><td><code>[source,target]</code></td><td><code>witness</code>, <code>witness_name</code>?</td></tr><tr><td><code>Src{cell}</code> / <code>Tgt{cell}</code></td><td><code>src</code>/<code>tgt</code></td><td><code>[cell]</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>RecordProj{record,field}</code></td><td><code>record_proj</code></td><td><code>[record]</code></td><td><code>field</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>RecordWith{record,field,value}</code></td><td><code>record_with</code></td><td><code>[record,value]</code></td><td><code>field</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Tag{tag,value}</code></td><td><code>tag</code></td><td><code>[value]</code></td><td><code>tag</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>Cobind{grade,body}</code></td><td><code>cobind</code></td><td><code>[grade,body]</code></td><td></td></tr><tr><td><code>Opaque{data}</code></td><td>— <em>leaf</em></td><td><code>[]</code></td><td>self-describing key</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>Self-describing leaf keys</strong> replace the spec&#39;s global <code>_LeafRegistry</code> (a named wart — process-global mutable state whose decode depended on registration order). A leaf term serializes to its own JSON <code>Term</code> encoding as the key, so <code>to_term</code> decodes it standalone; an unrecognized key decodes to <code>Term::Opaque</code>, keeping the round-trip total.</p><h2 id="module-union-find" tabindex="-1">Module: union_find <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-union-find" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: union_find”">​</a></h2><p>Union-find (disjoint-set): path halving + union by rank.</p><p>A straight port of the spec&#39;s <code>UnionFind</code> — the rank rule decides which root survives a merge, so canonical-id choice is identical to the Python version (higher rank wins; first argument on a tie).</p><h2 id="module-value" tabindex="-1">Module: value <a class="header-anchor" href="#module-value" aria-label="Permalink to “Module: value”">​</a></h2><p>Attribute values: the hashable, totally-ordered scalar/composite domain carried by e-node attributes.</p><p>Ports the Python spec&#39;s two-tier attr semantics:</p><ul><li><strong>Identity is spelling-strict.</strong> The spec&#39;s <code>ENode._sig</code> repr-keys every attr value, so <code>0</code> and <code>0.0</code> (and <code>0.0</code> and <code>-0.0</code>) are <em>distinct</em> enode payloads. <code>AttrValue</code>&#39;s <code>Eq</code>/<code>Hash</code> mirror that: floats compare by bit pattern, ints never equal floats.</li><li><strong>Matching is numerically lenient.</strong> The spec compares pattern attrs to node attrs with <code>!=</code> — Python&#39;s value equality, where <code>1 == 1.0</code>, <code>True == 1</code>, and <code>(1, &quot;a&quot;) == (1.0, &quot;a&quot;)</code>. That is [<code>AttrValue::match_eq</code>]: recursive numeric leniency, including the bool↔int coercion the Python tower admits.</li></ul><p>Ordering is <em>structural</em>, not repr-derived — the deliberate fix for the spec&#39;s <code>repr(attrs)</code> sort keys. The invariant the spec protects is a total, process-independent member order; structural <code>Ord</code> provides it directly (and without recomputing a repr per compare).</p>`,45)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};